    "id": "superexam-pathology-0023",
    "subject": "pathology",
    "originalNumber": 23,
    "stem": "A 25-year-old man presents with hematuria,\nperiorbital edema, hypertension, and\nhemoptysis. He has also experienced nausea,\nvomiting, fever, and chills. Serologic testing is\npositive for antiglomerular basement\nmembrane antibodies. Which of the following is\nthe classic histologic finding in this renal\ndisease?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Linear immunofluorescence"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "“Lumpy-bumpy” immunofluorescence"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "“Spike and dome” appearance of the\nglomerular basement membrane"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Subendothelial immune complex deposition"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "Tram-track appearance of the glomerular\nbasement membrane on electron microscopy"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "A",
    "explanation": "SIMILAR TO PREVIOUS BOARD EXAM\nCONCEPT/PRINCIPLE. The clinical description is that\nof Goodpasture syndrome (antiglomerular basement\nmembrane disease), caused by antibodies directed\nagainst antigens in the glomerular and pulmonary\nalveolar basement membranes. Because antigens are\nan intrinsic component of the basement membrane,\nlabeled antibodies “paint” the surface of the basement\nmembrane, resulting in the characteristic linear\nimmunofluorescent pattern characteristic of this\ndisorder. “Lumpy-bumpy” immunofluorescence is\nvery coarse, granular immunofluorescence found in\npoststreptococcal immune complex deposit disease.\nThe tram-track appearance is seen in\nmembranoproliferative glomerulonephritis. The\n“spike and dome” appearance is seen in membranous\nglomerulonephritis. Subendothelial immune complex\ndeposition is seen in lupus nephropathy.",
