    "id": "superexam-pathology-0025",
    "subject": "pathology",
    "originalNumber": 25,
    "stem": "A 45-year-old man presents with involuntary\nfacial grimaces and movements of the fingers.\nHis mother had had similar symptoms\nbeginning at about the same age. Her disorder\nhad progressed to dancing movements,\nwrithing of the arms and legs, and eventually\ncoma and death. His maternal grandfather had\nhad a similar disorder but at an age older than\nthe mother. Which of the following is most\ncharacteristic of this disease?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Degeneration of upper and lower motor\nneurons"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Dopamine depletion and depigmentation of\nthe substantia nigra"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Increased number of trinucleotide repeats in\na gene on chromosome 4"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Neurofibrillary tangles and amyloid plaques\nin the cerebral cortex"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "Pick bodies, characterized by round\nintracytoplasmic inclusions consisting of\nneurofilaments"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "This is a case of Huntington disease, which is an\nautosomal dominant, fatal, progressive degeneration\nand atrophy of the striatum (caudate nucleus and\nputamen). The disorder is characterized by an\nincreased number of trinucleotide (CAG) repeats in\nthe HD (huntingtin) gene on the short arm of\nchromosome 4. Degeneration of the upper and lower\nmotor neurons is characteristic of ALS. Dopamine\ndepletion and depigmentation of the substantia nigra\nis characteristic of Parkinson disease. Neurofibrillary\ntangles and amyloid plaques are found in Alzheimer\ndisease. Pick bodies can be found in Pick disease,\nwhich clinically resembles Alzheimer disease.",
