    "id": "superexam-pathology-0028",
    "subject": "pathology",
    "originalNumber": 28,
    "stem": "A 28-year-old woman complains of fatigue,\ndyspnea, and malaise. She also notes that her\nurine has been reddishbrown, particularly with\nthe first void of the morning. Subsequent\nstudies confirm that she has paroxysmal\nnocturnal hemoglobinuria. Which of the\nfollowing best describes the defect leading to\nthis condition?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Anti-intrinsic factor antibodies"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Deficiency of the intracellular structural\nprotein spectrin"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Impaired synthesis of the cell-surface GPI\nanchor"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Ineffective erythropoiesis"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "Substitution of a valine for a glutamate\nresidue in the β-globin gene"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "SIMILAR TO PREVIOUS BOARD EXAM\nCONCEPT/PRINCIPLE. Paroxysmal nocturnal\nhemoglobinuria results in an acquired\nintracorpuscular defect in the ability to synthesize GPI\nanchors required for appropriate placement of\ncomplement regulatory proteins on the surface of red\nblood cells. Functional deficiency of such proteins as\nCD55 and CD59 renders the cells sensitive to\ncomplement-mediated lysis. Anti-intrinsic factor\nantibodies are seen in pernicious anemia. Spectrin is\ndeficient in hereditary spherocytosis. Ineffective\nerythropoiesis is seen in megaloblastic anemia due to\nfolate or vitamin B12 deficiencies. Substitution of\nvaline for glutamic acid in the β-globin gene underlies\nthe defect in sickle cell anemia.",
