    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "Berry aneurysms, which occur in 10% to 15% of\npatients with adult polycystic kidney disease, are\nsmall saccular lesions that develop at the site of\ncongenital weakness of cerebral arteries, especially\nthose of the circle of Willis. Rupture of these\naneurysms is the most common cause of subarachnoid\nhemorrhage. Arteriovenous fistulas are often\nsecondary to trauma. Dissecting aneurysm is\nassociated with hypertension or with diseases\naffecting the vascular media, most notably Marfan\nsyndrome. Syphilitic (luetic) aneurysm is associated\nwith tertiary syphilis.",
