    "id": "superexam-pathology-0033",
    "subject": "pathology",
    "originalNumber": 33,
    "stem": "For the past week, a 65-year-old woman has\nbeen treated for a severe infection with broad-\nspectrum antibiotics, and she had recovered\nwell. Over the past day, however, she has\ndeveloped foul-smelling, voluminous, greenish,\nwatery diarrhea, as well as abdominal pain and\nfever. Which of the following is the mechanism\nassociated with this condition?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Aggregation of bacterial colonies on the\nlumen, forming pseudomembranes"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Bacterial release of exotoxin, inducing\nnecrosis of the mucosa"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Physical invasion of bacteria into the\nsuperficial mucosa, leading to\npseudomembrane formation"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Selective killing of C. difficile bacteria by\nantibiotics"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "Spread of the previous infection to the colon"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "Pseudomembranous colitis is caused by overgrowth of\nC. difficile. This organism produces exotoxin that\ninduces necrosis of the superficial mucosa, leading to\npseudomembrane formation. The bacteria itself does\nnot invade the mucosa. This condition most often\noccurs in patients with a history of broad-spectrum\nantibiotic use, because elimination of normal\nintestinal flora promotes overgrowth of C. difficile.",
