    "id": "superexam-pathology-0265",
    "subject": "pathology",
    "originalNumber": 265,
    "stem": "A patient with Duchenne muscular dystrophy is\na muscular disorder involving the dystrophin\ngene that usually leads to a male patient being\nwheelchair-bound during his teenage years. In\ncontrast, Becker muscular dystrophy is a milder\nbut similar form of disease wherein afflicted\npatients can usually still walk at the same age\nperiod. This is an example of:",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Pleiotropy"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Variable expressivity"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Anticipation"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Incomplete penetrance"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "Allelic heterogeneity"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "E",
    "explanation": "Allelic heterogeneity refers to different mutations at\nthe same locus. Variable expressivity is the same\nmutation but with different phenotypic\ncharacteristics. Anticipation occurs in trinucleotide\nrepeats and is characterized by an ealier and more\nsevere onset of disease in suceeding generations.\nIncomplete penetrance means some patients with the\ndisease causing mutation may not present signs or\nsymptoms; usually in autosominal dominant.\nPleiotropy occurs in mitochondrial disorders.",
